          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which progenitor cell gives rise to red blood cells, platelets, and granulocytes</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which progenitor cell gives rise to red blood cells, platelets, and granulocytes?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Common lymphoid progenitor (CLP)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The CLP gives rise to T-lymphocytes, B-lymphocytes, and natural killer cells — not red blood cells, platelets, or granulocytes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Proerythroblast</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The proerythroblast is already committed to the erythroid (red cell) lineage only. It cannot produce platelets or granulocytes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Common myeloid progenitor (CMP)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The CMP sits between the HSC and all myeloid lineages, giving rise to erythrocytes, platelets, neutrophils, monocytes, eosinophils, and basophils.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Haematopoietic stem cell (HSC)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The HSC is upstream of both progenitor families. While it ultimately produces all blood cells, the direct progenitor for red cells, platelets, and granulocytes is the common myeloid progenitor.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The haematopoietic stem cell gives rise to two progenitor families. The common myeloid progenitor (CMP) produces red blood cells, platelets, granulocytes, and monocytes. The common lymphoid progenitor (CLP) produces T cells, B cells, and NK cells."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The haematopoietic stem cell divides into two progenitor families: the CMP (red cells, platelets, granulocytes, monocytes) and the CLP (T cells, B cells, NK cells). Lineage commitment is driven by transcription factors and cytokines."
                }
              ],
