          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What does the appearance of band cells and metamyelocytes in peripheral blood in</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What does the appearance of band cells and metamyelocytes in peripheral blood indicate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Parasitic infection</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Parasitic infections typically cause eosinophilia (a raised eosinophil count), not a left shift with immature granulocytes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Overwhelming bacterial infection</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. This 'left shift' occurs when severe bacterial infection forces the marrow to release immature granulocytes — band cells and metamyelocytes — before they are fully mature.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chronic inflammation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Chronic inflammation is associated with monocytosis and tissue macrophage activation, not the release of immature granulocyte precursors into peripheral blood.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bone marrow aplasia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Marrow aplasia causes pancytopenia — reduced counts across all cell lines. The marrow has too few cells to release, so immature granulocytes would not flood the circulation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The presence of band cells and metamyelocytes in peripheral blood is called a 'left shift.' It signals overwhelming bacterial infection that has exhausted the marrow's reserve of mature segmented neutrophils, forcing release of immature forms."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "A 'left shift' — band cells and metamyelocytes in peripheral blood — is a hallmark of severe bacterial infection. The marrow's mature neutrophil reserve is overwhelmed, forcing premature release of cells that have not yet fully segmented."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
