          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A coagulation profile including prothrombin time and aPTT is requested. Which co</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A coagulation profile including prothrombin time and aPTT is requested. Which colour-coded tube should be used?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Purple tube (EDTA)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>EDTA chelates calcium, which coagulation factors depend on. A purple tube would make prothrombin time and aPTT unmeasurable.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Green tube (lithium heparin)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Heparin itself extends clotting times. The laboratory cannot distinguish tube heparin from therapeutic heparin, rendering coagulation results unreliable.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Grey tube (sodium fluoride)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Sodium fluoride inhibits glycolysis to preserve glucose levels. It is designed for glucose estimation, not coagulation studies.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Blue tube (sodium citrate)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Sodium citrate reversibly binds calcium, preserving the coagulation cascade for accurate PT and aPTT measurement when calcium is added back in the laboratory.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Coagulation tests require the blue (sodium citrate) tube. EDTA permanently chelates calcium needed by clotting factors, heparin prolongs clotting times independently, and fluoride tubes are designed for glucose preservation — none are suitable for coagulation profiles."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The blue sodium citrate tube is the only appropriate choice for coagulation studies. Citrate reversibly chelates calcium, which is restored during the assay. Remember the order of draw: blood cultures first, then blue (citrate) before other tubes."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
